    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d697">

                <pb facs="0628" n="619" xml:id="pg619"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 European War 1039/6752: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Algiers</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Cole</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Algiers</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1940-11-18T15:00:00+01:00">November 18,
                            1940—3 p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received November 19—8:09 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>167. Department’s rush 91.<anchor n="91a" xml:id="d697-f1"/> My 166.<anchor
                        n="92" xml:id="d697-f2"/> Message was delivered at noon this day. The only
                    comment by the General immediately followed the words concerning the
                    preservation of French African colonies against aggressions when he interposed
                    “and against British aggressions too”.</p>
                <p>At the conclusion of the short interview he requested that I should convey to the
                    American Government his appreciation of its interest in the matter and indicated
                    he would make an early reply.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Cole</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
